<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>reflection-at-curved-mirror</title></head><body><h1>reflection-at-curved-mirror</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Mirror Formula</li><li>Real and Virtual Images</li><li>Mirror Formula</li></ul><h2>Mirror Formula</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A spherical reflecting surface acts as a spherical mirror. For paraxial rays, a concave mirror converges parallel rays to a real focus; a convex mirror makes them diverge from a virtual focus.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The centre of curvature C is distance R from pole P; paraxial focus F is halfway between, so f=R/2. Wide-angle marginal rays do not meet this approximation exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Using Cartesian distances positive to the right, a concave mirror has f=−15 cm. For u=−30 cm, 1/v=1/f−1/u=−1/15+1/30=−1/30, so v=−30 cm and m=−v/u=−1.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Construct an image with a ray parallel to the principal axis (reflected through F for a concave mirror) and a ray through C (retraces itself). Their intersection locates a real image; backward extensions locate a virtual image.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Spherical aberration occurs when marginal rays focus differently from paraxial rays. A smaller aperture reduces it by excluding marginal rays.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Keep mirror signs consistent with the stated convention.</td></tr><tr><td>R is measured from pole to centre of curvature.</td></tr><tr><td>Aperture reduction suppresses marginal-ray aberration.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not substitute the thin-lens equation for the mirror equation.</td></tr><tr><td>f=R/2 assumes a spherical reflecting surface and small angles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Curved-mirror image formation follows reflection geometry. The paraxial focus lies halfway to the centre of curvature, and signed equations agree with ray constructions.</p><hr><h2>Real and Virtual Images</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A real image forms where reflected rays physically converge. It can be projected onto a screen and is commonly inverted for a single concave-mirror image of a real object beyond F.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>A virtual image forms where reflected rays only appear to originate when extended backward. It cannot be projected on a screen; a plane mirror forms an upright virtual image behind the mirror.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>For a concave mirror, an object beyond F generally gives a real image; an object between pole and F gives a virtual, upright, magnified image. A convex mirror gives a virtual, upright, diminished image for a real object.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Ray test: follow reflected rays forward. If they meet, the image is real; if only backward extensions meet, it is virtual. This geometric test is more reliable than judging whether the image seems behind the mirror.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Example: a shaving mirror used close to the face has the face inside the concave mirror’s focal length, producing an upright virtual magnified image.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Real rays meet; virtual rays do not.</td></tr><tr><td>Object position controls mirror image type.</td></tr><tr><td>A screen test distinguishes a real image.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Virtual describes ray geometry, not image brightness.</td></tr><tr><td>State the equation’s sign convention when giving image distances.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Ray geometry defines image type: physical convergence forms a real image, while backward extensions form a virtual image. This predicts screen projection and orientation.</p><hr><h2>Mirror Formula</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>A spherical reflecting surface acts as a spherical mirror. For paraxial rays, a concave mirror converges parallel rays to a real focus; a convex mirror makes them diverge from a virtual focus.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>The centre of curvature C is distance R from pole P; paraxial focus F is halfway between, so f=R/2. Wide-angle marginal rays do not meet this approximation exactly.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Using Cartesian distances positive to the right, a concave mirror has f=−15 cm. For u=−30 cm, 1/v=1/f−1/u=−1/15+1/30=−1/30, so v=−30 cm and m=−v/u=−1.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Construct an image with a ray parallel to the principal axis (reflected through F for a concave mirror) and a ray through C (retraces itself). Their intersection locates a real image; backward extensions locate a virtual image.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Spherical aberration occurs when marginal rays focus differently from paraxial rays. A smaller aperture reduces it by excluding marginal rays.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Keep mirror signs consistent with the stated convention.</td></tr><tr><td>R is measured from pole to centre of curvature.</td></tr><tr><td>Aperture reduction suppresses marginal-ray aberration.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Do not substitute the thin-lens equation for the mirror equation.</td></tr><tr><td>f=R/2 assumes a spherical reflecting surface and small angles.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Curved-mirror image formation follows reflection geometry. The paraxial focus lies halfway to the centre of curvature, and signed equations agree with ray constructions.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Mirror Formula</td><td>Curved-mirror image formation follows reflection geometry. The paraxial focus lies halfway to the centre of curvature, and signed equations agree with ray constructions.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Real and Virtual Images</td><td>Ray geometry defines image type: physical convergence forms a real image, while backward extensions form a virtual image. This predicts screen projection and orientation.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Mirror Formula</td><td>Curved-mirror image formation follows reflection geometry. The paraxial focus lies halfway to the centre of curvature, and signed equations agree with ray constructions.</td></tr></table></body></html>